import{U as e,W as t,nt as n,t as r}from"./chunks/framework.CPHJXLir.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Tables, Pages, Codeunits and Enums","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"tables-fields-and-business-data/index.md","filePath":"tables-fields-and-business-data/index.md"}`),a={name:`tables-fields-and-business-data/index.md`};function o(r,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),e(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="tables-pages-codeunits-and-enums" tabindex="-1">Tables, Pages, Codeunits and Enums <a class="header-anchor" href="#tables-pages-codeunits-and-enums" aria-label="Permalink to “Tables, Pages, Codeunits and Enums”">​</a></h1><p>In Business Central, AL development revolves around different types of objects. Each object serves a particular purpose, and understanding these objects will help you understand how extensions are structured.</p><p>Let&#39;s look at four important ones.</p><h2 id="tables" tabindex="-1">Tables <a class="header-anchor" href="#tables" aria-label="Permalink to “Tables”">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>table</strong> defines the structure of business data and stores its records.</p><p>Tables contain fields, which define the individual pieces of information stored for each record.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> A <code>Course</code> table might store information about training courses.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Course Code</th><th>Course Name</th><th style="text-align:right;">Price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CS101</td><td>Introduction to Programming</td><td style="text-align:right;">1500</td></tr><tr><td>CS102</td><td>Database Fundamentals</td><td style="text-align:right;">2000</td></tr><tr><td>CS103</td><td>Web Development</td><td style="text-align:right;">2500</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In this example:</p><ul><li>The table is <code>Course</code>.</li><li>The columns represent fields.</li><li>Each row represents a record.</li></ul><p><strong>Think of a table as the place where your business data lives.</strong></p><p>Other examples of tables in Business Central include Customer, Vendor, Item, and Sales Header.</p><h2 id="pages" tabindex="-1">Pages <a class="header-anchor" href="#pages" aria-label="Permalink to “Pages”">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>page</strong> provides the user interface through which users view and interact with business data and functionality.</p><p>Pages can display records, accept user input, and provide actions for performing tasks.</p><p>For example, a Course List page could display the courses stored in our <code>Course</code> table.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Course Code</th><th>Course Name</th><th style="text-align:right;">Price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CS101</td><td>Introduction to Programming</td><td style="text-align:right;">1500</td></tr><tr><td>CS102</td><td>Database Fundamentals</td><td style="text-align:right;">2000</td></tr><tr><td>CS103</td><td>Web Development</td><td style="text-align:right;">2500</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A user could open the page in Business Central, view the courses, and—if the page and permissions allow it—create or edit records.</p><p>Common page types include:</p><ul><li><strong>List:</strong> Displays multiple records.</li><li><strong>Card:</strong> Focuses on one record.</li><li><strong>Document:</strong> Presents a business document, such as a sales order.</li><li><strong>Role Center:</strong> Provides a workspace with information and links relevant to a user&#39;s role.</li></ul><p><strong>Think of a page as the window through which users interact with business data.</strong></p><p>The table stores the data; the page makes it accessible through the interface.</p><h2 id="codeunits" tabindex="-1">Codeunits <a class="header-anchor" href="#codeunits" aria-label="Permalink to “Codeunits”">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>codeunit</strong> is an AL object used to organize and execute business logic.</p><p>Unlike a page, which primarily provides an interface, a codeunit focuses on performing operations.</p><p>For example, imagine we want to calculate a discount for a training course.</p><p>We could place the discount-calculation logic in a codeunit so that different parts of our application can reuse it.</p><p>Other examples of tasks a codeunit could perform include:</p><ul><li>Calculating order totals.</li><li>Processing business transactions.</li><li>Sending notifications.</li><li>Validating business rules.</li><li>Coordinating operations across multiple tables.</li></ul><p>A codeunit can contain procedures and other supported AL constructs. Its logic can be invoked by pages, other codeunits, event subscribers, or other parts of the application.</p><p><strong>Think of a codeunit as a container for reusable business logic.</strong></p><p>For example, our course catalogue might eventually have a codeunit responsible for calculating course fees and discounts.</p><h2 id="enums" tabindex="-1">Enums <a class="header-anchor" href="#enums" aria-label="Permalink to “Enums”">​</a></h2><p>An <strong>enum</strong>, short for <em>enumeration</em>, defines a set of named values from which a field or variable can select.</p><p>Enums are useful when a value must belong to a predefined set of choices.</p><p>For example, each training course might have a status.</p><p>We could define a <code>Course Status</code> enum with these values:</p><ul><li>Draft</li><li>Published</li><li>Archived</li></ul><p>A course&#39;s status could then be set to one of those defined values rather than accepting arbitrary text such as <code>&quot;Still Working On It&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;Available Soon&quot;</code>.</p><p>Enums are commonly used with table fields to represent statuses, categories, and other predefined choices.</p><p><strong>Think of an enum as a predefined set of valid options.</strong></p><p>Other examples include payment status, approval status, and shipment status.</p><h2 id="quick-summary" tabindex="-1">Quick summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#quick-summary" aria-label="Permalink to “Quick summary”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Primary purpose</th><th>Example in our course catalogue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Table</strong></td><td>Defines and stores business data</td><td>Stores course names, codes, and prices</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Page</strong></td><td>Provides a user interface</td><td>Displays courses in Business Central</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Codeunit</strong></td><td>Organizes and executes business logic</td><td>Calculates course discounts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Enum</strong></td><td>Defines a set of named values</td><td>Defines Draft, Published, and Archived statuses</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="how-they-work-together" tabindex="-1">How they work together <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-they-work-together" aria-label="Permalink to “How they work together”">​</a></h3><p>Imagine we&#39;re building our training-course application:</p><ol><li>A <strong>table</strong> stores each course and its details.</li><li>An <strong>enum</strong> defines the valid statuses a course can have.</li><li>A <strong>page</strong> lets users view and manage courses.</li><li>A <strong>codeunit</strong> performs operations such as calculating discounts.</li></ol><p>Together, these objects help us build an organized Business Central extension.</p><p>One final distinction worth remembering: these are different AL object types, not four mandatory components of every extension. A small extension might use only a page extension and a codeunit, for example, while a larger application may use all four alongside many other objects.</p><p>Other important objects worth looking at are:</p><ul><li><strong>Report</strong>: processes and presents data, often for printing or analysis.</li><li><strong>Query</strong>: defines a structured way to retrieve data.</li><li><strong>XMLport</strong>: handles structured data import and export.</li></ul>`,51)]])}var s=r(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};